WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.150
Disagreement can reveal information a group needs.

2
00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:07.772
It can also become damaging when people attack one another or stop listening.

3
00:00:07.972 --> 00:00:13.408
Cedar Crossing colleagues disagree about long historical captions versus a simpler display.

4
00:00:13.608 --> 00:00:18.836
One wants to preserve context; the other wants visitors to understand the main point quickly.

5
00:00:19.036 --> 00:00:25.098
Those underlying concerns create more room for problem-solving than the demand that one person win.

6
00:00:25.298 --> 00:00:31.484
The chapter describes competing, accommodating, avoiding, compromising, and collaborating responses.

7
00:00:31.684 --> 00:00:34.776
Their usefulness depends on the issue and conditions.

8
00:00:34.976 --> 00:00:43.931
Collaboration explores a solution that addresses important interests, but it takes time and is not a universal response to every urgent situation.

9
00:00:44.131 --> 00:00:49.573
A compromise shares concessions; it may be workable without fully satisfying either concern.

10
00:00:49.773 --> 00:00:54.039
Listen to the evidence, clarify the decision, and agree how to move forward.

11
00:00:54.239 --> 00:00:57.602
Test a small display sample if that would inform the choice.

12
00:00:57.802 --> 00:01:03.556
The proposal is hypothetical: we still need evidence about what visitors can understand and use.
